// Core types.
//
// Khudra's core types are built into the language rather than declared: the
// checker knows their widths and their rules, and the VM knows their runtime
// representation. This file is where they are documented, so `lib/` describes
// the whole surface a program can rely on.
//
// Numbers
//   int8  int16  int32  int64      signed, aliased i8 i16 i32 i64
//   uint8 uint16 uint32 uint64     unsigned, aliased u8 u16 u32 u64
//   byte                           an alias of uint8
//   int                            an alias of int32
//   float                          32-bit floating point
//   dfloat                         64-bit floating point
//
//   Integer literals default to int32 and take a narrower or wider type from
//   context. Overflow wraps. Nothing widens or narrows on its own -- move
//   between widths with khuStdMath.convertTo (see lib/math.khu).
//
// bool
//   Exactly `true` or `false`. Conditions must be bool; no other type is
//   implicitly truthy.
//
// string
//   An immutable reference to text. String literals live in the bytecode
//   image's constant pool.
//
// Array
//   A reference to an array. It has no element type yet, so it can be held,
//   passed and compared but not indexed -- typed containers arrive with
//   generics (docs/roadmap.md).
//
// *T
//   A raw pointer. Never traced by the collector.
//
// MemoryAllocationTypeObject
//   The object that selects how a class is allocated. It has exactly two
//   factories:
//
//     MemoryAllocationTypeObject.setStandard()   garbage collected (default)
//     MemoryAllocationTypeObject.setManual()     manual, released with free
//
//   A class declares one as a field, and it is read once, at allocation time:
//
//     public MemoryAllocationTypeObject type = MemoryAllocationTypeObject.setStandard();
//
//   An allocation site may override the class default with a leading `manual`
//   or `standard` token -- `FrameBuffer(manual)`. Reassigning the field after
//   construction is a type error: the strategy is fixed at materialization.
//   See docs/memory-model.md.
//
// null
//   Means "not instantiated yet", never "empty". It fits any reference slot.
